PARTS = {
"G": ("Trained-transformer comparisons and hybrids", "Trained transformers are the natural comparison for compiled ones. Here we ask whether noise-aware training can make them tolerant, how their tolerance decays with length when properly trained, and whether a frozen digital counter bolted onto a transformer helps."),
"H": ("Wild ideas", "Three out-of-the-box ideas were tried at small scale before the systematic study of Part I: replicate state units, superpose codes to compress the state, and store several programs in one associative memory with sparse error-correcting codes. All three are negative at the sizes tested, and the third was redone properly in Part I (where one error of the first attempt is corrected)."),
"J": ("LLM-synthesized transformers: protocol, harness and first (contaminated) run", "From here the question changes from *how do we build a robust network* to *how robust are networks built by a language model, compared with other construction methods*. Part J describes the frozen protocol, the shared evaluation harness and a first run by the same Claude instance that wrote the harness (therefore *contaminated*). It is a pointer, not a result."),
"K": ("LLM-synthesized transformers: fresh-session run (uncontaminated), first pass", "The same prompts, given to fresh chat sessions that had seen nothing about the project. The project owner ran 60 chats; we re-evaluated every submission with the frozen harness. **One of the four conditions (feedback) was compromised by an operator error, and the model identity of five chats is unresolved; both are described in K0.**"),
"L": ("Theory exploration: a first-order predictor of failure levels", "After the benchmark data existed we asked whether anything *explains* where failures occur. This part is a record of a sequence of pre-registered predictors, including the ones that failed and an artifact we had to retract. It is the confirmatory part of the report; the earlier parts are mostly exploratory."),
"M": ("Prior-art audit", "A literature check of every claim in this report, done late and with limited search. It decides what we present as reproduction (known work) and what looks new."),
}
SUB = {
"G1": ("Can noise-aware training make a trained transformer tolerant?",
 "At N=16 it raises relative tolerance to alpha50 0.196 at clean accuracy 0.967 (baseline about 0.075 at 0.993); at N=32 clean accuracy is only 0.68. It trades exactness for tolerance and does not fix the scaling. One seed."),
"G2": ("A hybrid: transformer plus frozen digital counters.",
 "The hybrid reaches clean accuracy 1.000 at N=16, 32 and 64 with alpha50 0.068, 0.046, 0.034; the transformer alone, properly trained for 8,000 steps, reaches 0.995, 0.993, 0.875 with alpha50 0.08 to 0.086, 0.046 to 0.048, 0.036. The hybrid's lasting benefit is exactness at long lengths, not tolerance. An earlier claim that trained transformers lose tolerance strongly with length is weaker once they are trained properly (about N to the minus 0.6 to minus 0.8). The `reverse` experiment at N=32 is inconclusive."),
"H1": ("Replication, superposition, and noise-trained bilinear weights.",
 "Replicating every state unit with unscaled weights gives only about sqrt(r) tolerance gain at r squared parameters. Superposition codes need 96 to 192 dimensions to decode 51 one-hot states exactly (no compression). Noise-injection training of the bilinear weights broke exactness (65 percent of one-step transitions correct; length-400 accuracy 0.23) and did not raise tolerance. All negative."),
"H2": ("Error-correcting sparse state codes (Willshaw transitions), and one memory for several programs.",
 "(A) Sparse constant-weight codes with binary Willshaw associations were verified exact but needed 2 to 15 times more parameters than the one-hot design and gave similar sigma50. The statement that no exact code exists for majority at N=32 and 64 was later shown to be an artifact of the readout used (see Part I, I2). (B) Storing several different programs in one associative memory found no exact shared memory up to 256 state units for 2, 4, 8 programs. Negative at these sizes."),
"I0": ("Audit closure: which gaps in the plan were closed.", "A checklist mapping each requirement to what was done and what was not (for example, zero-protected attacks at N=32 were not run)."),
"I1": ("Weight-tied composition: certificates, bisection, per-head versus joint attacks.",
 "Certificates for the tied-bilinear and the original ReLU-gated tied design are tight (first failure at 1.5 to 2.3 times the certified radius for bilinear and 2.1 to 3.4 times for ReLU) and independent of the number of heads k. The weakest head breaks at about the same budget as any chosen head, and one shared perturbation breaks all k heads at once: shared role matrices are a shared failure mode. N=32 attacks with k of 15 and 30 used a reduced-strength attack."),
"I2": ("Error-correcting state codes under a full noise matrix.",
 "Replication buys tolerance to activation noise (1.5 to 3.3 times) at the price of tolerance to parameter noise and 9 to 25 times the parameters; the certified activation tube is unchanged. Willshaw-type codes are exact but worse on most axes and 2 to 8 times larger than one-hot; two-hot grid codes never gave an exact automaton. A single noise burst is survived at 1.8 to 10 times (typically 2 to 3 times) the magnitude that the same noise repeated every step can be; a burst that lands on a wrong-but-valid state is never corrected. The switching slack divided by the row norm predicts activation sigma50 (Spearman 0.96 across clustered cells, so a consistency check, not a test)."),
"I3": ("A fast-weight universal interpreter: construction and exactness.",
 "One shared set of slow weights executes arbitrary different programs exactly, with up to 16 programs in a single memory. But the memory needed grows linearly with the number of stored transitions (key width about half the number of transitions, for every set of programs and every number of states), the shared memory is within about 10 percent of the sum of separate memories, and the interpreter does not exploit structure shared between programs: for template programs a role-dictionary tied design needs 1.4k to 2.6k parameters against 21.7k fast parameters for the interpreter. Execution costs 10 to 150 times more multiply-accumulates than an independent network. This is shared execution, not shared capacity."),
"I4": ("Robustness against the number of programs, and separated perturbation targets.",
 "Slow-weight, fast-weight and joint perturbations fail at different levels, and joint noise is worse than either. Fresh noise is not worse than static noise for slow weights. Perturbing the slow weights *before* the programs are written is fully absorbed (the programs are written through the defective interpreter), whereas the same perturbation after writing breaks execution at about 0.1. Adding programs lowers per-program fast-weight tolerance only mildly (about 20 percent from 4 to 16 programs), and independent networks are more robust than the interpreter in the joint metric."),
"I5": ("Certificates and attacks for the interpreter.",
 "The certified radius shrinks about five times as programs are added from 1 to 16 (fast 8.9e-3 to 1.9e-3; slow 6.2e-3 to 1.2e-3), roughly like P to the minus 0.6. Worst-case radii are about 50 times below Gaussian tolerance (the L-infinity worst case sees the L1 norm of the key where Gaussian noise sees the L2 norm). The attack-found failure is 4 to 10 times the certified radius, so this certificate is looser than for the tied designs (the attack is weak, so these are upper bounds)."),
"I6": ("Composition and sequential execution on one shared memory.",
 "Seven pipelines (the readout of one program is the input of another) on one memory are all exact and lose almost no tolerance relative to the programs run alone. Programs can be added online only with rehearsal of the old ones: with a plain delta rule on the new tape only, earlier programs do not survive beyond the first additions. An Adam-based write gave zero survivors from the first addition (a worse case caused by Adam's per-weight step normalization)."),
"I7": ("Answers to the ten questions of the plan.", "A compact answer sheet; see the table in this section."),
"I8": ("Caveats for Part I.", "Small scale (n up to 21 states for the scaling study), reduced-strength attacks in places, coarse grids, one key seed."),
"J0": ("What prior work exists on LLMs synthesizing network weights?", "A three-query check (not an audit). Direct LLM weight synthesis exists in early forms; we found no robustness comparison."),
"J1": ("What was built?", "Six tasks, one transformer family in which every parameter is a named perturbable tensor, and one evaluation pipeline used for every network: a clean-correctness gate (exhaustive and hidden test set), static noise protocols, a new sample-certified interval bound for transformers, and an attack."),
"J2": ("The contaminated run: 16 constructions written before the checker was run.", "All 16 passed the exhaustive correctness gate on the first try. Because the author also wrote the harness and had seen the project, this says little about LLM capability in general."),
"J3": ("Pre-registered expectations versus this run.", "Failures were not common (E1 not supported), sigma50 fell near the compiled range (E3 supported), certificate and attack relations were partly as expected (E6)."),
"J4": ("Observations and competing explanations.", "Constructions share a mechanism (hard attention and BOS-based counting with piecewise-linear lookups), which may explain why `hist` and `reverse` match Tracr. The attention query/key groups are often harmless; embeddings, the value/output path and zero-entry noise limit the networks."),
"J5": ("Limitations of the contaminated run.", "One LLM, one attempt per cell, small tasks, sample-based certificate, weak attack."),
"K0": ("Deviations and open items (read first).", "The feedback condition was compromised (the feedback checker was most likely run with the wrong condition flag for the unconstrained cells), five chats used a different model whose identity was not recorded, and transcripts and per-attempt records are missing for the feedback and literal-weight chats. We report these results but keep them out of the feedback comparison and from any model attribution."),
"K1": ("Clean-correctness gate.", "57 of 60 first answers were exactly correct on all 335,922 inputs."),
"K2": ("Category 3b (LLM code computes the weights), no feedback: robustness per attempt.", "Absolute sigma50 spans 6e-4 to 0.12. Attempts of one cell differ by up to 38 times; several cells are tightly reproducible."),
"K3": ("Category 4 (literal weights).", "Feasible for the two smallest tasks (10 of 12 correct); attribution to a model is pending, see K0."),
"K4": ("Feedback cells.", "Reported but compromised: the first answers were all correct, and later revisions in two cells changed the architecture and became invalid."),
"K5": ("Pre-registered expectations versus the fresh run.", "E1 not supported; E2 (large spread) supported in 2 of 12 cells; E3 not supported for the counter tasks; E6 partly."),
"K6": ("Contaminated versus fresh, and convergence.", "The two runs agree on the simplest tasks (for `length` constrained the three fresh attempts and the contaminated one have bit-identical weights) and differ for the counters. Convergence to one design is the exception except for the simplest task."),
"K7": ("Limitations of the fresh run.", "Chat interface with unknown sampling, one model family, at most 3 attempts per cell, feedback compromised."),
}
